# @desktop — minimal KDE Plasma 6 Wayland (plan/03, plan/09). Marked entries are filtered out
# by stage 20's filter_set_file before portage ever sees the set: `#printing` / `#cjk` when
# INCLUDE_PRINTING / INCLUDE_CJK_FONTS are 0.
#
# THERE IS NO `#not-live` MARKER ANY MORE (plan/34 §6, was plan/20). A live-role profile's tree
# has to be the desktop's tree plus a tail, at identical versions — plan/34 §3 turns the tail
# into a systemd system extension built as a DIFFERENCE against the desktop's, and a marker that
# drops a desktop package from a live build is a deletion that difference cannot represent. Every
# atom below now emerges on every profile that names this set, live or not.
#
# This is a 1:1 functional mirror of the GNOME set it replaces, not a full Plasma install:
# session + compositor + settings + file manager + terminal + software centre, and nothing
# else. Applications are Flatpaks (plan/03), so there is no Ark, no Kate, no Okular and no
# Gwenview here for exactly the reasons file-roller and gnome-text-editor were dropped from
# the GNOME set: each is a preinstalled Flatpak (`FLATPAK_PREINSTALL` in build.conf), and each
# would cost a native dependency tail on a read-only root to save a Flatpak download. Spectacle,
# KDE Partition Manager and KInfoCenter (below, near dolphin/konsole) are the three exceptions,
# because for them it isn't a choice: none has ever been published to Flathub.
kde-plasma/plasma-desktop
kde-plasma/plasma-workspace
kde-plasma/kwin
kde-plasma/systemsettings
kde-plasma/plasma-systemmonitor
kde-plasma/plasma-nm
kde-plasma/plasma-pa
kde-plasma/powerdevil
kde-plasma/bluedevil
kde-plasma/kscreen
kde-plasma/polkit-kde-agent
kde-plasma/kwallet-pam
# Emerged on every profile now, live included (plan/34 §6) — through 0.3.1 this carried
# `#not-live` (plan/20 §4.3), on the argument that an app store is not worth its weight on a
# stick discarded in twenty minutes. plan/34 §6 removed the marker: the live medium's own tree
# now has to be a superset of the desktop's at identical versions, so Discover ships there too.
# It is not wasted even on the medium: the live session's own Flatpak installs are the source the
# install copies from once Phase D lands (plan/34 §9), so Discover is how someone adds an app to
# the machine being installed before the disk is even written.
#
# TWO PACKAGES, and the second one is the resolver's answer rather than this comment's:
# kde-plasma/discover itself (~3.3 MiB) and kde-frameworks/purpose, orphaned because Discover was
# its only consumer in this closure. Everything else Discover names — appstream, attica,
# knewstuff, kirigami-addons, kstatusnotifieritem, kidletime, qcoro — STAYS, because something
# else in the closure already wanted it. dev-libs/appstream is the one worth naming: it looks
# like Discover's dependency and is plasma-workspace's, which RDEPENDs >=dev-libs/appstream-1[qt6]
# outright whenever its own `appstream` USE flag is on (config/portage/package.use/image).
#
# That ratio — two packages out of eight plausible ones — is why plan/20 §2.7 says the orphan set
# has to be DERIVED and not read off an ebuild. It went the other way for Spectacle, where two
# named packages took nineteen. Read the removals off installer.lock, not off this line.
#
# The layout script beside it (config/calamares/system/lookandfeel) has called Discover out by
# name since plan/16, because the Icons-Only Task Manager pins it by KConfigXT default. That was
# always an argument made in a comment; this is the same argument made in the set, and the two
# are not redundant — the pin has to be rewritten whether or not the package is installed, since
# KService drops an unresolvable launcher silently rather than leaving a hole.
#
kde-plasma/discover
kde-plasma/xdg-desktop-portal-kde
kde-plasma/breeze
# The GTK theming bridge, kept so Flatpak apps (Firefox is preinstalled) do not render as an
# unstyled GTK island. It is also the reason "remove all GNOME" cannot be literal: gtk
# hard-RDEPENDs adwaita-icon-theme, which RDEPENDs gnome-base/librsvg. See plan/09 §4 for the
# full enumerated residue — dropping these two packages removes almost all of it.
kde-plasma/breeze-gtk
kde-plasma/kde-gtk-config
# Emerged on every profile now, live included (plan/34 §6). 216.8 MiB of artwork, and through
# 0.3.1 it carried `#not-live`: the single most expensive tree in the image per byte downloaded
# — PNG and JPEG, so EROFS gives back 1%, not the 44% it averages over everything else (plan/10
# §5) — dropped on a medium that boots once to run Calamares and has no use for a wallpaper
# PICKER. plan/34 §6 restored it (and stage 50 no longer deletes Breeze's own `Next` alongside
# it either): the live medium's own branded wallpaper, installed by stage 40, is still what the
# live session's layout script and lock screen actually point at — this collection ships beside
# it now rather than being the reason it had to.
kde-plasma/plasma-workspace-wallpapers
kde-plasma/plasma-login-manager  # the display manager; its System Settings KCM is built in
# /usr/share/wayland-sessions/plasma.desktop lives HERE, not in the display manager and not in
# plasma-workspace. It is a PDEPEND of plasma-workspace so it would arrive anyway, but its
# wayland/X flags are the real lever deciding whether an Xorg session exists at all
# (X? pulls kde-plasma/kwin-x11), so it is named explicitly.
kde-plasma/plasma-login-sessions
kde-frameworks/breeze-icons
kde-apps/dolphin
kde-apps/konsole
# Native because Flathub has no Spectacle: flathub/org.kde.spectacle carries only an unmerged
# "beta" branch (5 open PRs, 1 open issue, never released) — no `org.kde.Spectacle` or
# `org.kde.spectacle` app has ever shipped. kde-plasma/, not kde-apps/: the ebuild moved
# category upstream. Pulls one dependency not already in the image: media-libs/opencv
# (unconditional RDEPEND, used for the region auto-detect on capture). Everything else it
# needs — kpipewire, layer-shell-qt, prison, the xcb libs — is already pulled in by
# plasma-workspace/kwin's own screencast support. Pinned tree has 6.6.6 stable; 6.6.6-r1 and
# 6.7.4-r1 are both ~amd64 only, so no keyword exception is taken here.
#
# Emerged on every profile now, live included (plan/34 §6). Through 0.3.1 this carried
# `#not-live`, on the OpenCV line above: Spectacle itself is 2.8 MiB and media-libs/opencv is
# 41.5, and dropping the atom on a live medium dropped three packages nothing else in the
# closure reached (kquickimageeditor and opencv both went with it, 45.0 MiB installed). plan/16's
# own standing rule was already the argument against it, not for it: a failed install is exactly
# when someone most wants a screenshot of the error, and a phone photo of the screen is not a
# substitute for a PNG off the medium before it is unplugged. plan/34 §6 restores it for that
# reason as much as for the superset one.
kde-plasma/spectacle
# Native for the same reason as Spectacle above: no `org.kde.partitionmanager` (or any casing)
# has ever shipped on Flathub — plausibly because a disk partitioner is a bad sandbox fit in
# the first place. sys-block/, not kde-apps/ or kde-plasma/. IUSE is empty; the only dependency
# not already in the image is sys-libs/kpmcore, the backend library — no libparted pulled in,
# it drives sys-apps/util-linux (already in @base) directly. Privilege escalation goes through
# sys-auth/polkit-qt, already here for Discover/systemsettings.
sys-block/partitionmanager
# Native for the same reason as the two above: no `org.kde.kinfocenter` (or any casing) has
# ever shipped on Flathub, and the reason plausibly rhymes with Partition Manager's — a tool
# whose entire job is reading the host's hardware is a poor sandbox fit. kde-plasma/, not
# kde-apps/: it releases with Plasma, not with the applications bundle. Pulls nothing the image
# does not already carry — the relock adds exactly one atom: every framework it links —
# kcmutils, kdeclarative, kirigami, kitemmodels, kio, systemsettings — is already in the
# closure for System Settings itself, and everything its KCMs read — sys-apps/pciutils,
# sys-apps/hwdata, virtual/libusb, media-libs/mesa, x11-libs/libdrm — is already in
# @base/@hardware. kde-frameworks/kdoctools is build-time only, because -handbook is global
# USE (make.conf.in). 2.2 MiB installed over 387 files; the pinned tree carries 6.6.6 stable
# (6.7.5 is ~amd64 only), so no keyword exception is taken here. The ebuild's elog names
# optional query tools its panels can exec — dmidecode, wayland-utils, mesa-progs, clinfo,
# vulkan-tools, xdpyinfo — and none of them is added: each is a probe for one panel, not a
# dependency, and a probe the image lacks costs that panel's data, not the tool.
kde-plasma/kinfocenter
kde-apps/kio-extras              # the gvfs equivalent: trash, network, thumbnails
kde-plasma/print-manager         #printing
sys-power/power-profiles-daemon
media-video/pipewire
media-video/wireplumber
x11-base/xwayland
media-fonts/noto
media-fonts/noto-emoji
media-fonts/noto-cjk             #cjk
net-print/cups                   #printing

# ---- managed mode (plan/19 Phase D) ---------------------------------------------------------
# The System Settings module. From config/portage/overlay, not from ::gentoo — a Plasma KCM is a
# compiled C++ plugin and there is no QML-only path into System Settings, so this is the one
# thing in managed mode that cannot be a script in config/rootfs.
#
# The "distro" token is rebranded by filter_set_file the same way it is in filenames, so this
# line follows DISTRO_ID and nothing here needs editing to rename the distro.
#
# It costs no NEW dependency: everything it links against (Qt6, kcmutils, ki18n, kirigami,
# systemsettings) is already in this set's closure for Plasma itself.
#
# Emerged on every profile now, live included (plan/34 §6). Through 0.3.1 this carried
# `#not-live`: a live medium is never enrolled, and the module answers "which policy is applied,
# when did it last sync, who manages this" for a session that enrols nothing and is discarded in
# twenty minutes. It shows up in a live session's Kickoff now, reporting "not enrolled" —
# harmless (plan/34 §10), and the superset invariant leaves no other option once the KCM is
# desktop's and the installer tree has to be the desktop's plus a tail.
#
# NOT the whole of managed mode, and the distinction still matters. The installer's accounts page
# (distro-base/distro-calamares-accounts, in config/portage/sets/installer) is installer-ONLY
# and stays exactly where it is: it is how the machine being INSTALLED gets enrolled, which is
# the one managed-mode job a live medium genuinely has.
distro-base/distro-kcm-managed
